---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import { skinRecord, recordKinds } from '../data/skin-record';

const lastReviewedISO = '2026-11-15';
const lastReviewed = new Date(lastReviewedISO).toLocaleDateString('en-US', {
  year: 'numeric', month: 'long', day: 'numeric', timeZone: 'UTC',
});

const groups = recordKinds.map((kind) => ({
  ...kind,
  entries: skinRecord.filter((entry) => entry.kind === kind.id),
}));

const jsonLd = {
  '@context': 'https://schema.org',
  '@type': 'CollectionPage',
  name: 'The skin visual record',
  dateModified: lastReviewedISO,
  description:
    'Citations for published skin photographs that bear on melanocortin pigment, mole change, and approved-drug endpoints. Journal figures are not hosted.',
};
---
<Base
  title="The skin visual record — Tan Lines"
  description="Which published skin photographs belong in the record: mole change, Addisonian pigment, approved-drug endpoints, and a Fitzpatrick reference. Gray-market before-and-afters are excluded."
>
  <script type="application/ld+json" set:html={JSON.stringify(jsonLd)} />
  <article class="prose record">
    <p class="eyebrow">The Record</p>
    <h1>The skin visual record</h1>
    <p class="lede">
      A photograph helps this site only when a reader can say what it shows,
      who published it, and what it is not allowed to prove. The entries below
      are that list. The pixels of identifiable patients stay with the journals.
    </p>
    <p class="reviewed">Last reviewed {lastReviewed}.</p>

    <h2>What a pair has to carry</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>The same person, the same site, and two dates.</li>
      <li>Lighting and distance close enough that a darker pixel is the skin, not the room.</li>
      <li>A citation, a license note, and one sentence on the claim the picture supports.</li>
      <li>For a mole: whether anyone biopsied it, and what the slide said.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      A forum “after” with no interval and a product name in the caption fails
      every line. Those images are not collected.
    </p>

    <figure>
      <svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" viewBox="0 0 780 168" role="img" aria-labelledby="tfit dfit">
        <title id="tfit">Fitzpatrick types I through VI as a schematic scale</title>
        <desc id="dfit">Six labeled color chips from very light to very dark. They are a vocabulary for the photoprotection brief, not photographs and not a before-and-after.</desc>
        <rect width="780" height="168" fill="#FBFAF7"/>
        <rect x="8" y="8" width="764" height="152" rx="10" fill="none" stroke="#E4DFD6"/>
        <text x="28" y="36" font-family="'Spectral',Georgia,serif" font-size="16" font-weight="600" fill="#17130E">Fitzpatrick I–VI, drawn, not photographed</text>
        <g>
          <rect x="28" y="52" width="108" height="64" rx="6" fill="#F3D7C3"/>
          <text x="82" y="140" text-anchor="middle" font-family="'Archivo',sans-serif" font-size="12" fill="#5A4F44">I</text>
          <rect x="150" y="52" width="108" height="64" rx="6" fill="#E4B894"/>
          <text x="204" y="140" text-anchor="middle" font-family="'Archivo',sans-serif" font-size="12" fill="#5A4F44">II</text>
          <rect x="272" y="52" width="108" height="64" rx="6" fill="#C99262"/>
          <text x="326" y="140" text-anchor="middle" font-family="'Archivo',sans-serif" font-size="12" fill="#5A4F44">III</text>
          <rect x="394" y="52" width="108" height="64" rx="6" fill="#A06A3C"/>
          <text x="448" y="140" text-anchor="middle" font-family="'Archivo',sans-serif" font-size="12" fill="#FBFAF7">IV</text>
          <rect x="516" y="52" width="108" height="64" rx="6" fill="#6E4228"/>
          <text x="570" y="140" text-anchor="middle" font-family="'Archivo',sans-serif" font-size="12" fill="#FBFAF7">V</text>
          <rect x="638" y="52" width="108" height="64" rx="6" fill="#3C2418"/>
          <text x="692" y="140" text-anchor="middle" font-family="'Archivo',sans-serif" font-size="12" fill="#FBFAF7">VI</text>
        </g>
      </svg>
      <figcaption>
        A reference scale, not a result. Type I burns and does not tan; type VI
        rarely burns. The original melanocortin photoprotection brief was written
        for the light end of this scale. These chips are not spectrophotometry
        and they are not anyone’s skin.
      </figcaption>
    </figure>

    <figure>
      <svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" viewBox="0 0 780 220" role="img" aria-labelledby="tadd dadd">
        <title id="tadd">Sites of Addisonian pigmentation, as a diagram</title>
        <desc id="dadd">A schematic marks creases, scars, gums, and sun-exposed skin as the places excess ACTH darkens. No patient is depicted.</desc>
        <rect width="780" height="220" fill="#FBFAF7"/>
        <rect x="8" y="8" width="764" height="204" rx="10" fill="none" stroke="#E4DFD6"/>
        <text x="28" y="40" font-family="'Spectral',Georgia,serif" font-size="16" font-weight="600" fill="#17130E">Where the high melanocortin state writes itself</text>
        <text x="28" y="64" font-family="'Archivo',sans-serif" font-size="13" fill="#5A4F44">Addison’s. No drug. The diagram names sites; it is not a portrait.</text>
        <g font-family="'Archivo',sans-serif" font-size="13" fill="#17130E">
          <rect x="28" y="92" width="166" height="78" rx="8" fill="#F4EFE6" stroke="#E4DFD6"/>
          <text x="111" y="136" text-anchor="middle">Palmar creases</text>
          <rect x="214" y="92" width="166" height="78" rx="8" fill="#F4EFE6" stroke="#E4DFD6"/>
          <text x="297" y="136" text-anchor="middle">Scars</text>
          <rect x="400" y="92" width="166" height="78" rx="8" fill="#F4EFE6" stroke="#E4DFD6"/>
          <text x="483" y="136" text-anchor="middle">Gums and mucosa</text>
          <rect x="586" y="92" width="166" height="78" rx="8" fill="#F4EFE6" stroke="#E4DFD6"/>
          <text x="669" y="136" text-anchor="middle">Sun-exposed skin</text>
        </g>
      </svg>
      <figcaption>
        Pigment from an unopposed melanocortin signal, in a disease. See
        <a href="/articles/too-much-too-little/">Too much, too little</a>.
        Teaching photographs of patients are not hosted.
      </figcaption>
    </figure>

    {groups.map((group) => (
      <section>
        <h2>{group.label}</h2>
        {group.entries.length === 0 && group.id === 'reference-scale' && (
          <p>
            The Fitzpatrick figure above is the reference. It is original to
            this page. It is not a before-and-after, and it is not a photograph.
          </p>
        )}
        <ul class="entries">
          {group.entries.map((entry) => (
            <li id={entry.id}>
              <h3>{entry.title}</h3>
              <p>{entry.claim}</p>
              <p><strong>Not a claim.</strong> {entry.notAClaim}</p>
              <p class="cite">
                {entry.href ? <a href={entry.href}>{entry.citation}</a> : entry.citation}
              </p>
              <p class="host">{entry.hosting}</p>
            </li>
          ))}
        </ul>
      </section>
    ))}

    <h2>Left out on purpose</h2>
    <p>
      Influencer before-and-afters, forum progress threads, nasal-spray
      diaries, and any pair that implies a dose, a schedule, or a seller.
      Lighting is uncontrolled, the interval is whatever the caption says,
      and on this domain those pictures would work as advertising. The rule
      is journalism about the gray market, never a guide to it.
    </p>
    <p>
      The mole entries are the ones that change a clinical argument. Read
      them next to <a href="/articles/the-mole-problem/">The mole problem</a>.
      A new or changing mole is a reason to see a dermatologist, not a reason
      to compare yourself with a case report.
    </p>
  </article>
</Base>

<style>
  .record { max-width: 46rem; }
  .reviewed {
    font-family: var(--font-sans, 'Archivo', sans-serif);
    font-size: 0.85rem;
    color: var(--ink-soft);
  }
  .entries { list-style: none; padding: 0; }
  .entries li {
    margin: 0 0 1.5rem;
    padding: 1rem 0 0;
    border-top: 1px solid var(--line, #E4DFD6);
  }
  .entries h3 {
    font-family: var(--font-display);
    font-size: 1.2rem;
    margin: 0.6rem 0;
  }
  .cite { font-size: 0.92rem; }
  .host {
    font-size: 0.85rem;
    color: var(--ink-soft);
  }
</style>
